<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Józef Piłsudski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jozef Pilsudski</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Józef Piłsudski was a Polish revolutionary who led the Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legions</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and sat on the Provisional Council of State in 1917 before being interned by the Germans. In November 1918 he took power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> as chief of state of the new Second Polish Republic, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> in 1920 and ruled as strongman after his coup of 1926.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jozef-pilsudski.jpg" alt="Józef Piłsudski" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:J%C3%B3zef_Pi%C5%82sudski_(-1930).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dictators also ruled Poland after <b>Józef Piłsudski</b>’s coup of 1926, Portugal under António de Oliveira Salazar, Spain under Francisco Franco from 1939, much of the Balkans and the Baltic states, and Brazil under Getúlio Vargas from 1937.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Dąbrowski's name was at times replaced by later leaders, from Józef Chłopicki to <b>Józef Piłsudski</b> and Władysław Sikorski.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li><li><blockquote>A Provisional Council of State, including <b>Jozef Pilsudski</b>, was set up in 1917, but real power remained with the German governor-general in Warsaw.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1916-1918">Kingdom of Poland (1916-1918)</a></li><li><blockquote>As the partitioning empires collapsed in November 1918, <b>Jozef Pilsudski</b> took power in Warsaw from the Regency Council of the Kingdom of Poland (1916-1918), and independence was proclaimed on 11 November.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-polish-republic">Second Polish Republic</a></li></ul></section>
